{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cacb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a809-8791-7622-9a9b-78a086ec7f4f"},"sequence":960,"recorded_at":1780531400001915,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"8b868cf4-3c1a-4266-acd2-1e63eb0638f1","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"8b868cf4-3c1a-4266-acd2-1e63eb0638f1","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"8b868cf4-3c1a-4266-acd2-1e63eb0638f1"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a812-708b-7810-b3a0-52a5116a65fa"},"task_id":"01a0a812-708b-7810-b3a0-52a5116a65fa","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a812-708b-7810-b3a0-52a5116a65fa","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/relux-tunnel/LOGBOOK.md`.\n1|# Flight Logbook\n2|\n3|> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\n4|> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\n5|\n6|## 2026-08-30\n7|\n8|### Shared-runtime Story landing gates recovered (TASK-260830-1x524u)\n9|\n10|- INTEGRITY: The five Shared Runtime Story checkpoint commits were re-materialized with valid configured-human signatures and monotonic Asia/Tbilisi timestamps. Local and remote `task-board/story/STORY-260715-1y04r0` now match at `9bf4d0892db03035b989a4c040cfee37636ff8df`; all prior Story children and the direct packet-bridge, SSH-engine, and generated-project architecture blockers are terminal.\n11|- LANDING GATES: The candidate preserves the existing serial Codex/Sol high spawn policy and adds the fail-closed `make credential-free-validate` Story integration suite. The owning Epic dependency was narrowed to the accepted macOS architecture verification. Scoped board reads show this Task and Story unblocked with every prior direct child terminal; the current board-wide validator returns exit `0` while reporting 368 `MISSING_ACTIVITY` records outside the current Task/Story streams, so the handoff preserves that anomaly instead of backfilling transaction-owned activity or claiming a globally clean board.\n12|- BOUNDARY: Revalidation changes no runtime behavior and does not add live provider wiring. The generated macOS `PacketTunnelProvider` remains a lifecycle shell; validation is limited to deterministic harnesses, compile/build gates, documentation, and read-only system-state observations, with no provider launch, VPN activation, route mutation, or DNS mutation.\n13|- VALIDATION REWORK: The first configured completion attempt failed closed because the managed worktree resolved the default sibling legacy checkout inside `.temp`; its command now derives the control repository through Git's absolute common directory. The next attempt exposed a stale “valid” scheme fixture that omitted the two UI-test schemes already required by the production exact-set gate; the fixture and all isolated missing/unexpected rows now use the complete active graph. Narrowing the production gate by only `ReluxProxyIOSUITests` makes the named contract test exit `1`; byte-identical restoration returns it to `0`.\n14|- FINAL EVIDENCE: The corrected credential-free completion suite, eight focused runtime suites (105 tests), six harness tests plus seven fixtures, 32 M0 binding negatives, both platform adapter builds, unsigned macOS targets, 52 local documentation links, and four byte-identical PlantUML SVG renders pass. The full 514-test/44-suite Swift run passes with the 25 explicitly unavailable ReluxNIOSSH rows; selected Shared Runtime/M1 coverage is 90.34% lines and 84.33% regions, and package coverage is 92.92% lines and 85.22% regions.\n15|- PRIVACY OBSERVATION: Broad route-table hashes changed during long build runs and are retained as inconclusive dynamic-state evidence, not a pass. A bounded route-socket monitor around a fresh production-entry harness run recorded 35 `RTM_MISS` lookup events and zero `RTM_ADD`, `RTM_DELETE`, `RTM_CHANGE`, `RTM_NEWADDR`, or `RTM_DELADDR` mutations; full-tunnel route selectors, NetworkExtension configuration, DNS state, and the zero Relux process set matched before and after.\n16|\n17|### Accepted M0 production bindings (TASK-260720-1qhxqa)\n18|\n19|- DECISION: Bound the exact reviewer-accepted generated-project, packet/HEV, and SSH outcome/verdict resource pairs plus the accepted M1 runtime contract in `Configuration/TASK-260720-1qhxqa_m0-production-bindings-v1.json`. Acceptance timestamps preserve upstream date precision instead of inventing a time of day.\n20|- PERMIT: `productionCompositionPermitted=true` is only a recomputed result. `scripts/validate-m0-production-bindings.py` fails closed on digest drift, absence, malformed input, unknown schema/fields, stale/superseded evidence, a narrowed input set, negative/missing compatibility rows, and checked-in graph/native-pin drift.\n21|- BINDINGS: macOS-only generated graph; MTU/buffers/batching/HEV values 1500/32768/64/5ms/24576/4096/2/500 with no HEV fork; selected pinned libssh2/OpenSSL adapter with exact algorithm, window, rekey, lifecycle, notice, maintenance, and revalidation constraints. Deferred iOS, MTU 4096/8500, whole-extension memory, general UDP, lanes, reconnect, and M3 tuning remain blocked/deferred.\n22|- CONSUMER: `TASK-260715-3ejhyy` must use the machine manifest as its sole M0 binding source and validate it at the future macOS production factory entry point before concrete SSH/PacketFlowBridge/HEV construction or `NetworkSettingsApplier.apply`.\n23|- BOARD ANOMALY: Final `task-board validate` returned process exit 0 but printed two `PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` rows. `STORY-260715-2ungml` is unrelated; current `STORY-260715-1y04r0` remained `backlog` while this leaf was `development`. The leaf had `derivedBlockedBy=[]`, all four hard prerequisites were `done`, and the manifest/resource gates passed. The mismatch is retained as board-state evidence rather than misreported as a clean board validation.\n24|- REVIEW REWORK: CR revision 1 was correctly rejected because the validator admitted a mutant with MTU 1501, packet budget 1, removed notice obligations, and an `unchecked` compatibility condition. The rework pins SHA-256 `fda46421ae6a6c24cf58f5bfb520399b9ebc8d31800b7d61ed18a38231aea43e` over the complete canonical semantic contract. The exact reviewer mutant now exits 1 at `NORMALIZED-CONTRACT` with `productionCompositionPermitted=false`; the unchanged accepted manifest exits 0. The earlier trailing whitespace in the human-readable manifest was removed.\n25|- REWORK BOARD ANOMALY: `task-board validate` again returned process exit 0 while printing three `PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` rows: unrelated `STORY-260715-1zzt0c` and `STORY-260717-1ecq74`, plus owning `STORY-260715-1y04r0` stored `to-dev` versus child aggregate `development`. The leaf itself remains in the correct `development` role state with every hard prerequisite done. Raw output is attached and is not described as a clean board validation.\n26|- REVIEW REWORK 2: CR revision 2 was correctly rejected because substring-presence checks admitted an extra direct provider dependency while preserving all required tokens. `verify_repository` now normalizes the protected Swift `dependencies` and provider `resources` arrays and compares exact closures sourced from the immutable manifest; missing, duplicate, extra, and unknown entries fail at stable `REPOSITORY-GRAPH`. The production CLI negative suite covers extra dependencies independently for the provider, macOS adapter, native adapter, and selected SSH adapter, plus a duplicate provider dependency. The final run passes 14/14 tests; the unchanged authoritative baseline remains permitted.\n27|- REVIEW REWORK 3: CR revision 3 was correctly rejected because raw-source target lookup selected a commented decoy before the real provider declaration. Target and array resolution now use a lexical Swift view: comments cannot supply declarations or fields, string contents cannot supply code candidates, bracket matching ignores both, and duplicate real target declarations fail closed. Production-entry regressions cover the exact comment decoy, a multiline-string decoy, and an ambiguous duplicate target.\n28|- REVIEW REWORK 4 VALIDATION: The final suite passes 16/16. The unchanged authoritative manifest passes the production Make gate with SHA-256 `39b3428d6cf778fa6b682bb0853a177d6b85f03b8062309f0d198dcdfd49fae6` and permission true; the exact reviewer-attached comment mutant exits 1 at `REPOSITORY-GRAPH` with permission false. Owner and `TASK-260715-3ejhyy` sole-consumer attachments remain byte-identical. `task-board validate` returned process exit 0 while retaining three `PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` rows (owning Story stored `to-dev` versus child aggregate `development`, plus two unrelated Stories); the raw result is attached and is not represented as a clean validation.\n29|- REVIEW REWORK 5: CR revision 4 was correctly rejected because the pinned normalized contract omitted all eight accepted resource digests, allowing changed upstream bytes plus a synchronized mutable-manifest digest to retain permission. The independent canonical trust root now includes every M0 and M1 outcome/verdict SHA-256. The production-entry synchronized-rebind regression refuses at stable row `NORMALIZED-CONTRACT`; the full suite passes 17/17, all eight authoritative digests independently match, and the unchanged owner/repository/sole-consumer manifest remains byte-identical at SHA-256 `39b3428d6cf778fa6b682bb0853a177d6b85f03b8062309f0d198dcdfd49fae6` with permission true.\n30|- REVIEW REWORK 6: CR revision 5 was correctly rejected because the production gate did not bind the accepted libssh2 patch and public-header SHA-256 values to the checked-in native pin graph. The gate now validates the patch manifest and selected patch bytes plus every required-slice header lock and header bytes; missing, malformed, duplicate, or inconsistent records fail at stable `REPOSITORY-SSH-PIN`. Patch-lock and single-slice header-lock production mutants exit 1 with permission false; the full suite passes 20/20 and the unchanged baseline remains permitted.\n31|- REVIEW REWORK 7: CR revision 6 was correctly rejected because the new actual-byte gates lacked durable negative coverage. Production-entry regressions now flip one byte in the selected libssh2 patch and one byte in a required `libssh2.h` slice while leaving accepted locks unchanged; both exit 1 at stable `REPOSITORY-SSH-PIN` with `productionCompositionPermitted=false`. The full suite passes 22/22, the authoritative baseline remains permitted at manifest SHA-256 `39b3428d6cf778fa6b682bb0853a177d6b85f03b8062309f0d198dcdfd49fae6`, native dependency validation passes, and all eight upstream resource digests plus owner/sole-consumer attachment identity match. Board validation returned process exit 0 while retaining the unrelated `STORY-260715-1zzt0c` mismatch and owning Story stored `to-dev` versus child aggregate `development`; raw output is attached without calling it clean.\n32|- REVIEW REWORK 8: CR revision 7 was correctly rejected because duplicate root JSON keys could retain permission and the accepted SSH integrity projection omitted exact license/acknowledgements and retained-fork pins. Manifest and board-state parsing now reject duplicate keys at stable trust-boundary rows. The machine manifest copies the accepted libssh2 `COPYING`, OpenSSL license/acknowledgements, retained ReluxNIOSSH patch, and retained license SHA-256 values without reinterpretation; the independent canonical trust root and negative suite bind mutation/removal, while repository validation binds the checked-in selected-license and retained-manifest pins. The exact reviewer mutant now exits 1 at `MANIFEST-JSON` with permission false; the full suite passes 27/27, baseline production validation and native dependency validation exit 0, all eight upstream resources match, and repository/owner/sole-consumer manifests are byte-identical at SHA-256 `0deddb2a2692cecb17a0650741f74577c945600de97c9a41970b035b452f0361`. Board validation returned process exit 0 while retaining the unrelated `STORY-260715-1zzt0c` mismatch and owning Story stored `to-dev` versus child aggregate `development`; raw output is attached without calling it clean.\n33|- REVIEW REWORK 9: CR revision 8 correctly proved that unchanged `NativeDependencies/manifest.json` locks did not protect actual HEV XCFramework bytes. The immutable binding now carries the exact 16-file HEV path/SHA-256 map; production validation requires the checked-in lock map, complete artifact file set, and every actual file digest to match at stable `REPOSITORY-HEV-PIN`. Durable negatives mutate the real macOS archive, its lock, and the missing/extra file set while requiring exit 1 and `productionCompositionPermitted=false`. The corrected full suite passes 31/31, authoritative production validation permits the unchanged baseline at manifest SHA-256 `095372d61bd2baf46a72c701e0b94ee18cece67b8d51d445b30f50e2b5c39c73`, native dependency validation and lint exit 0, and board validation exits 0 while retaining two explicit parent-status mismatch rows. An earlier 30/30 test execution is retained as non-acceptance evidence because its zsh wrapper attempted to assign the read-only `status` variable and therefore exited 1 after the tests.\n34|\n35|### M0 packet bridge and HEV baseline accepted (TASK-260715-2jatnd)\n36|\n37|- DECISION: The macOS-first M0 Bridge gate passes under ADR-024 with MTU 1500, requested/effective 32768-byte socket buffers, 64-packet/5-ms pump budgets, HEV task-stack/TCP/UDP-copy values 24576/4096/2, and an initial measured 500-session ceiling. MTU 4096 remains injectable behind end-to-end proof; MTU 8500 is a named failed default candidate because constrained-buffer rows produced 768 `EMSGSIZE` refusals.\n38|- EVIDENCE: Public-API bridge, IPv4/IPv6 framing, deterministic faults, hostile-frame fuzzing, bounded pressure, lifecycle, packaging, notices, and physical Mac MTU/memory rows trace to accepted task-scoped resources in `docs/TASK-260715-2jatnd_m0-bridge-hev-decision-adr.md`. The worst measured incremental HEV/bridge cost was 9,715,712 bytes at 500 sessions; whole-extension SSH/DNS/relay/cache/reconnect memory remains unmeasured.\n39|- FORK: Unmodified HEV `ad7600497931205105b08367bd1b450048157e40` remains selected. There is no Instruments material-bottleneck evidence or improved callback prototype, so fork authorization is rejected by default.\n40|- GAPS: Physical iPhone, jetsam, NAT64, sleep/wake, energy, and whole-extension memory stay deferred/blocked and are never inferred from macOS. M1 consumers may use this baseline only through the exact accepted-outcome binding task; production composition remains fail-closed on its other M0 inputs.\n41|\n42|## 2026-08-27\n43|\n44|### 0824 — Deterministic lifecycle release is separated from Darwin residency (TASK-260715-135rr8)\n45|- ROOT CAUSE: The rev3 gate coupled two distinct claims. Harness-owned channels, reads, queues, boundary/native lifecycle counters, and descriptor-close stages were released deterministically, but the gate still required Darwin to return allocator pages during the same 500-cycle observation. One reviewer run therefore failed on a bounded `+180224 B` non-decreasing footprint rise while the next two passed.\n46|- FIX: The schema-2 lifecycle evidence now records an `ownedResourcesReleased` verdict and every underlying ownership field for each cycle. The production gate requires 500 cycles, zero failed owned-resource-release cycles, and the unchanged post-warmup maximum resident-footprint drawup ceiling of `256 KiB`. Monotonic and late rises remain classified with exact transition/cycle evidence, but OS page-return timing is no longer used as a proxy for product-owned release. The matrix atomically emits its exact-tree-bound raw report before returning a failing verdict.\n47|- NEGATIVE EVIDENCE: Production-call-site tests reject 499 cycles, a footprint overflow, and one missing owned-resource release at cycle 499. A separate emission-order test proves the raw-evidence closure runs before the production gate throws. Forged working-tree and HEV artifact provenance remain fail-closed.\n48|- EXPLORATORY MEASUREMENT: A fresh exact-tree 500-cycle loopback probe reproduced the allocator ambiguity with `+147480 B` net change and `180248 B` maximum drawup while all `500/500` cycles restored every owned resource. Descriptors returned from 9 to 3, threads from 9 to 7, and the deterministic release gate passed. This supports signal separation without widening the 500-cycle or 256 KiB bounds.\n49|\n50|### 0751 — Lifecycle observer allocation removed before classification (TASK-260715-135rr8)\n51|- ROOT CAUSE/FIX: `measureRealHEVLifecycle` retained every sample in an initially empty Swift `Array` after taking its baseline. The observer now reserves the exact bounded cycle count before the baseline snapshot. A production-call-site regression asserts capacity for 1000 samples, and the diagnostic entry accepts only 100/500/1000 cycles; malformed or wider values fail closed.\n52|- MEASUREMENT: Corrected exact-tree loopback probes ran 100/500/1000 real HEV cycles. Post-warmup net change was `+81,944 B` / `-212,992 B` / `-32,768 B`; maximum drawup was `98,328 B` / `65,536 B` / `65,536 B`; increase/equal/decrease transitions were `3/85/1`, `4/479/6`, and `7/977/5`. The prior late approximately 16 KiB jumps above cycle 1000 disappeared; the last 1000-cycle increase was cycle 481. Every sample restored live sessions, reads, and queues to zero; descriptors ended at 3 and threads at 8.\n53|- RULE: A fresh production-entry repeat proved 100 cycles insufficient: it stopped fail-closed on one `+65,536 B` increase and no release. The simplest repeatable gate therefore uses the next predeclared row, 500 cycles, after fixed 10-sample warmup. It permits a full plateau or a measured release only while maximum drawup stays within 256 KiB (1% of the upper 25 MiB target); late unreleased rise and a released-but-over-ceiling linear sawtooth fail closed. The earlier 2000-cycle/trailing-1000 rule measured observer allocation and is retired rather than widened further.\n54|\n55|### 0732 — Extended lifecycle converges to a bounded HEV/runtime envelope (TASK-260715-135rr8)\n56|- INVESTIGATION: A bounded process-local 2000-cycle SwiftPM/loopback run captured every physical-footprint jump and 100-cycle segment. The sampler observed both rises and releases, including `-1,196,032 B` and `-1,130,496 B` segments; post-warmup net change was `-2,310,120 B`. Across the last 500 samples, net change was `-65,536 B` and range was `98,304 B`. Descriptors stayed `3`, threads stayed within `6...8`, and every post-cleanup HEV session, read, and queue count was zero. Boundary start/stop and HEV start/main-return counters were exactly one per cycle. Earlier 500- and 1000-cycle repeats still had late rises, proving neither duration alone established convergence.\n57|- RULE: The production evidence gate now requires at least 2000 samples (20× the original 100-cycle minimum), the existing fixed 10-sample allocator warmup, post-warmup and trailing 1000-sample maximum drawup no greater than `256 KiB` (1% of the upper 25 MiB provisional envelope), and either a trailing plateau or an observed post-warmup release. Maximum drawup measures each upward excursion from the running minimum, so a large release is not itself a failure. Raw evidence records every parameter, transition count, jump cycle, ownership counter, and sample. Positive plateau and bounded-oscillation tests plus unreleased late-rise and oversized-high-water negative tests bind the classifier; the original rise-plus-plateau negative remains.\n58|- ANOMALY/FIX: The first extended attempt stopped at cycle 257 with Swift signal 5 because the test payload used checked `UInt8(iteration)`. `UInt8(truncatingIfNeeded:)` removes that artificial 255-cycle harness limit while address and port continue to identify each flow. The 2000-cycle investigation passed in 106.74 seconds. No VPN, NetworkExtension, route, DNS, interface, packet-filter, SSH, or global-memory-pressure operation ran.\n59|\n60|### 0716 — Correct lifecycle trend gate stops rev3 matrix (TASK-260715-135rr8)\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
